ameA. \subsection{Prompting Paradigms \& Approaches} We detail different prompting paradigms in Section~ef{sec:intro} and Table~ef{tab:schemes}. % There are numerous other works related to prompting. We now briefly summarize selected most related ones; more extensive descriptions can be found in dedicated surveys~\cite{wang2023interactive, lertvittayakumjorn2021explanation, wang2021putting, hartmann2022survey}. Wang et al.~proposed Plan-and-Solve, an approach to enhance CoT with an explicit planning stage~\cite{wang2023plan}. Using complexity-based criteria to enhance prompting within a CoT was designed by Fu et al.~\cite{wang2022self, fu2022complexity}. The self-taught reasoner (STaR)~\cite{zelikman2022star} generates several chain of thoughts, and selects the ones that are valid. Similarly, a scheme by Shum et al.~\cite{shum2023automatic} generates a pool of CoT candidates, and selects the best candidate based on whether the candidates match the ground truth and on a policy gradient-based method. Automatic prompt generation overcomes the issues of scaling in CoT~\cite{shin2020autoprompt, li2021prefix, lester2021power}. Zhou et al.~propose to harness selecting the best prompt out of a candidate set~\cite{zhou2022large}. % Skeleon-of-Thought~\cite{ning2023skeleton} generates at first a number of skeleton answers (brief bullet points of 3 to 5 words) and expands on these points in parallel in a second step. Finally, in prompt chaining, one cascades different LLMs. This enables prompting different LLMs via different contexts, enabling more powerful reasoning~\cite{creswell2022selection, nye2021show, wu2022ai, dohan2022language, qiao2022reasoning, wu2022promptchainer, wu2022ai}. 
ameAS is orthogonal to this class of schemes, as it focuses on a single context capabilities. \subsection{Self-Reflection \& Self-Evaluation} Self-reflection and self-evaluation were introduced recently~\cite{shinn2023reflexion, paul2023refiner, madaan2023self, xie2023decomposition, zhu2023solving}. They are used to enhance different tasks, for example for code generation~\cite{chen2023teaching} or computer operation tasks~\cite{kim2023language}. In 
ameA, we partially rely on self-evaluation when taking decisions on how to expand the graph of thoughts within a prompt. \subsection{LLMs \& Planning} There are many works recently on how to plan complex tasks with LLMs~\cite{huang2022language, zhang2023planning, yao2022react, yang2023foundation, wang2023describe, huang2022inner}. 
ameAS could be seen as a generic framework that could potentially be used to enhance such schemes, by offering a paradigm for generating complex graph-based plans. \if 0 \subsection{Combining LLMs with External Units} Another part of the LLM landscape is combining LLMs with different additional tools/agents. Examples include AutoGPT~\cite{richards2023auto} (augmenting GPT-4 with additional modules including an execution agent and a memory unit), HuggingGPT~\cite{shen2023hugginggpt} (making the LLM responsible for selecting various tools), training external entities to check the LLM outcomes~\cite{cobbe2021training}, using search trees augmented with external text~\cite{liu2023llm+}, or relying on a classical external planner~\cite{schlag2023large}. % 
ameAS could potentially be extended as a framework for harnessing the interactions between such interacting agents.  i \subsection{Graphs and Graph Computing} % Graphs have become an immensely popular and important part of the general computing landscape~\cite{lumsdaine2007challenges, malewicz2010pregel, gregor2005parallel, gregor2005lifting, sakr2020future}. Recently, there has been a growing interest in domains such as graph databases~\cite{robinson2015graph, besta2019demystifying, besta2023high, besta2023gdi, besta2022neural}, graph pattern matching~\cite{fan2010graph, cheng2008fast, teixeira2015arabesque, besta2022probgraph, besta2021sisa, besta2021graphminesuite}, graph streaming~\cite{feng2015distinger, dhulipala2019low, besta2019practice}, and graph machine learning as well as graph neural networks~\cite{hamilton2017representation, wu2020comprehensive, zhou2020graph, zhang2020deep, chami2020machine, hamilton2017representation, bronstein2017geometric, besta2021motif, gianinazzi2021learning, scarselli2008graph, besta2022parallel}. The graph abstraction has been fruitful for many modern research domains, such as social sciences (e.g., studying human interactions), bioinformatics (e.g., analyzing protein structures), chemistry (e.g., designing chemical compounds), medicine (e.g., drug discovery), cybersecurity (e.g., identifying intruder machines), healthcare (e.g., exposing groups of people who submit fraudulent claims), web graph analysis (e.g., providing accurate search services), entertainment services (e.g., predicting movie popularity), linguistics (e.g., modeling relationships between words), transportation (e.g., finding efficient routes), physics (e.g., understanding phase transitions and critical phenomena), and many others~\cite{lumsdaine2007challenges, cook2006mining, jiang2013survey, horvath2004cyclic, chakrabarti2006graph}. % In this work, we harness the graph abstraction as a key mechanism that enhances prompting capabilities in LLMs. \section{Conclusion} Prompt engineering is one of the central new domains of the large language model (LLM) research. It enables using LLMs efficiently, without any model updates. However, designing effective prompts is a challenging task. In this work, we propose 
ameS (
ameA), a new paradigm that enables the LLM to solve different tasks effectively without any model updates. The key idea is to model the LLM reasoning as an arbitrary graph, where thoughts are vertices and dependencies between thoughts are edges. This enables novel transformations of thoughts, such as aggregation. Human's task solving is often non-linear, and it involves combining intermediate solutions into final ones, or changing the flow of reasoning upon discovering new insights. 
ameAS reflects this with its graph structure. % % It also brings the LLM reasoning closer to that of a human being. 
ameAS outperforms other prompting schemes, for example ensuring 62\% increase in the quality of sorting over ToT, while simultaneously reducing costs by $>$31\%. % % Even more importantly, 
ameA's generic formulation based on arbitrary graphs opens a way to future novel powerful reasoning approaches that will benefit from the richness of graph structures. % We also propose a novel metric for a prompting scheme, the volume of a thought, to indicate the scope of information that a given LLM output could carry with it, where 
ameAS also excels. This provides a step towards more principled prompt engineering. The graph abstraction has been the foundation of several successful designs in computing and AI over last decades, for example AlphaFold for protein predictions. Our work harnesses it within the realm of prompt engineering. \small \section*{Acknowledgements} We thank Hussein Harake, Colin McMurtrie, Mark Klein, Angelo Mangili, and the whole CSCS team granting access to the Ault and Daint machines, and for their excellent technical support. % We thank Timo Schneider for help with infrastructure at SPCL. % This project received funding from the European Research Council (Project PSAP, No.~101002047), and the European High-Performance Computing Joint Undertaking (JU) under grant agreement No.~955513 (MAELSTROM). This project was supported by the ETH Future Computing Laboratory (EFCL), financed by a donation from Huawei Technologies. This project received funding from the European Union’s HE research and innovation programme under the grant agreement No. 101070141 (Project GLACIATION). 
