led, and ordered. Especially in the context of CoT, one does not have to provide intermediate thoughts explicitly, but rather attempt to generate them based solely on the input. In that case, for a single CoT, we would have $[y, a_1, a_2, ..., a_N] \sim p^{CoT}_	heta (y, a_1, a_2, ..., a_N | x)$ instead of $y \sim p^{CoT}_	heta (y | x, a_1, a_2, ..., a_N)$.  i \if 0 \subsection{Graph Theory} We use 	extbf{graph theory} to model the LLM though process. Here, a graph is a tuple $G=(V,E)$; $V$ is a set of vertices (nodes); $|V|=n$ and $|E|=m$. $G$ is directed and thus the edges are a subset of ordered vertex pairs $E\subseteq V	imes V$. % % $N_{in}(v)$ and $N_{out}(v)$ are in- and out-neighborhoods of a vertex~$v$, i.e., $N_{in}(v) = \{w \in V\ |\ (w,v) \in E \}$ and $N_{out}(v) = \{w \in V\ |\ (v,w) \in E \}$. Then, $d_{in}(v)$ and $d_{out}(v)$) are the corresponding in- and out-degrees. % A vertex models an LLM thought while and edge $(u,v)$ models the fact that $u$ is used to obtain $v$.  i \section{The 
ameAS Framework} We now detail the 
ameAS framework. We present it in Figure~ef{fig:schemes}, and compare it to other prompting strategies. Formally, 
ameAS can be modeled as a tuple $(G, \mathcal{T}, \mathcal{E}, \mathcal{R})$, where $G$ is the ``LLM reasoning process'' (i.e., all the LLM thoughts within the context, with their relationships), $\mathcal{T}$ are the potential thought transformations, $\mathcal{E}$ is an evaluator function used to obtain scores of thoughts, and $\mathcal{R}$ is a ranking function used to select most relevant thoughts. \subsection{Reasoning Process} We model the reasoning process as a directed 	extbf{graph} $G = (V, E)$; $V$ is a set of vertices and $E\subseteq V	imes V$ is a set of edges. % $G$ is directed and thus the edges are a subset of ordered vertex pairs $E\subseteq V	imes V$. % A vertex contains a \emph{solution} to a problem at hand (be it an initial, intermediate, or a final one). The concrete form of such a thought depends on the use case; it could be a paragraph (in writing tasks) or a sequence of numbers (in sorting). A directed edge $(t_1, t_2)$ indicates that thought $t_2$ has been constructed using $t_1$ as ``direct input'', i.e., by explicitly instructing the LLM to use $t_1$ for generating $t_2$. In certain use cases, graph nodes belong to different \emph{classes}. For example, in writing tasks, some vertices model \emph{plans of writing a paragraph}, while other vertices model \emph{the actual paragraphs of text}. In such cases, 
ameAS embraces a 	extbf{heterogeneous graph} $G = (V, E, c)$ to model the LLM reasoning, where $c$ maps vertices $V$ into their respective classes $C$ (in the above case, it would be $C = \{ plan, par \}$). Hence, any vertex $v$ can model different aspects of reasoning. % \if 0 % Most often, it forms a \emph{solution} to a problem at-hand (be it an initial, intermediate, or a final one), i.e., $c(v) = s$. The concrete form of such a thought depends on a specific use case. For example, it could be a paragraph (in writing tasks) or a sequence of numbers (in sorting tasks). On top of that, we also use vertices to model \emph{plans} of future actions; in which case $c(v) = p$. We found out that explicitly asking the LLM to plan future thoughts by generating a thought that contains this plan, enables more powerful reasoning. Overall, unless stated otherwise, we assume $C = \{s, p\}$. %  i We associate $G$ with the LLM reasoning process. To advance this process, one applies 	extbf{thought transformations} to $G$. An example of such a transformation is to merge best-scoring (so far) thoughts into a new one. Another example is to loop over a thought, in order to enhance it. Note that these transformations strictly extend the set of transformations available in the CoT, CoT-SC, or ToT. % % For example, a potential transformation for a ToT would be to backtrack from a given path of thought. We detail all thought transformations in Section~ef{sec:transf}. \subsection{Transformations of Thoughts} \label{sec:transf} 
ameAS enables novel transformations of thoughts thanks to the graph-based model for reasoning. We refer to them as 	extbf{graph-enabled transformations}. % For example, in writing, one could combine several input articles into one coherent summary. In sorting, one could merge several sorted subarrays of numbers into a final sorted array. % We illustrate examples of aggregation and generation in Figure~ef{fig:examples}.  egin{figure}[t] \centering % \vspaceSQ{-1em} \includegraphics[width=1.0\columnwidth]{examples.pdf} % \vspace{-0.5em} \caption{Examples of aggregation and generation thought transformations.} % \vspaceSQ{-1em} \label{fig:examples} \end{figure} Formally, each such transformation can be modeled as $\mathcal{T}(G, p_	heta)$ where $G = (V,E)$ is the graph reflecting the current state of the reasoning, and $p_	heta$ is the used LLM. $\mathcal{T}$ modifies $G$ usually by adding new vertices and their incoming edges. % We have $G' = \mathcal{T}(G, p_	heta) = (V', E')$, where $V' = ( V \cup V^+ ) \setminus V^-$ and $E' = ( E \cup E^+ ) \setminus E^-$. $V^+$ and $E^+$ are new vertices and edges inserted into $G$ to model the new thoughts and their dependencies, respectively. To maximize the expressiveness of 
ameAS -- we also enable the user to explicitly \emph{remove} thoughts, by specifying the corresponding vertices and edges to be removed ($V^-$ and $E^-$, respectively). % Here, it is the user's responsibility to ensure that the sets $V^+, E^+, V^-,$ and $E^-$ come with consistent transformations (i.e., for example, that the user does not attempt to remove a vertex that does not exist). % This enables seamless incorporation of schemes where, in order to save space within the context, one can remove parts of reasoning that do not promise improvements. The specific form of $\mathcal{T}$ and how it impacts $G$ depends on a specific transformation. We first detail the primary graph-enabled thought transformations, and then proceed to describe how 
ameAS embraces the transformations from the earlier schemes. Unless stated otherwise, $V^- = E^- = \emptyset$.  egin{figure*}[hbtp] \centering % \vspaceSQ{-1em} \includegraphics[width=1.0	extwidth]{arch_9.pdf} % \vspaceSQ{-1em} \caption{The system architecture of 
ameA, and the APIs of respective modules. The user can straightforwardly extend the design towards new prompting schemes, experiment with novel thought transformations, and plug in different LLMs. The 	extcolor{blue}{blue part of the figure contains the architecture overview}, 	extcolor{green}{the green part lists the API}, and 	extcolor{red}{the red part contains example prompts together with a GRS and operations involved}.} % \vspaceSQ{-1em} \label{fig:arch} \end{figure*} \subsubsection{Aggregation Transformations} First, with 
ameA, one can 	extbf{aggregate arbitrary thoughts} into new ones, to combine and reinforce the advantages of these thoughts, while eliminating their disadvantages. % % For example, in a writnig task, this could correspond to % In the basic form, in which only one new vertex is created, $V^+ = \{v^+\}$ and $E^+ = \{ (v_1, v^+), ..., (v_k, v^+) \}$, where $v_1, ..., v_k$ are the merged $k$ thoughts. % %\subsubsection{Convergence of Paths} % More generally, this enables 	extbf{aggregating reasoning paths}, i.e., longer chains of thoughts, beyond just individual thoughts. With the graph model, it is simply achieved by adding outgoing edges from the vertices $v_1, ..., v_k$, modeling final thoughts in several chains, into a single thought $v^+$ combining these chains. \subsubsection{Refining Transformations} Another thought transformation is the 	extbf{refining} of a current thought $v$ by modifying its content: $V^+ = \{\}$ and $E^+ = \{ (v, v) \}$. %This could be done after deciding that the current chain (with $v$ in it) is not going to improve with more steps, but using $v$'s contents could enhance $u$. This loop in the graph indicates an iterated thought with the same connections as the original thought. % can also iterate over the current thought. %Note that, formally, this class is a special case of aggregation, in which there is only one input vertex. \subsubsection{Generation Transformations} Finally, one can 	extbf{generate one or more new thoughts based on an existing single thought} $v$. This class embraces analogous reasoning steps from earlier schemes, such as ToT or CoT-SC. Formally, we have $V^+ = \{ v^+_1, ..., v^+_k \}$ and $E^+ = \{ (v, v^+_1), ... ,(v, v^+_k) \}$. \subsection{Scoring \& Ranking Thoughts} Thoughts are scored to understand whether the current solution is good enough. A score is modeled as a general function $\mathcal{E}(v, G, p_	heta)$, where $v$ is a thought to be evaluated. We use the state of the whole reasoning process ($G$) in $\mathcal{E}$ for maximum generality, because -- for example -- in some evaluation scenarios, scores may be relative to other thoughts. % \subsection{Ranking Thoughts} 
ameAS can also rank thoughts. We model this with a function $\mathcal{R}(G, p_	heta, h)$ where $h$ specifies the number of highest-ranking thoughts in $G$ to be returned by $\mathcal{R}$. While the specific form of $\mathcal{R}$ depends on the use case, we most often use a simple yet effective strategy where $h$ thoughts with the highest scores are returned, i.e., $v_1, ..., v_h = \mathcal{R}(G, p_	heta, h)$. Specific forms of $\mathcal{E}$ and $\mathcal{R}$ depend on the use case. We discuss the details in Section~ef{sec:cases}. For example, the score (or rank) for sorting corresponds to the count of elements correctly sorted (or incorrectly, when using the error as a score). \section{System Architecture \& Extensibility} \label{sec:arch} % The architecture extends that of ToT by Long~\cite{long2023large}. % The 
ameAS architecture consists of a set of interacting modules, see Figure~ef{fig:arch} 	extcolor{blue}{(the blue part)}. These modules are the {Prompter} (prepares the messages for the LLM), the Parser (extracts information from LLM thoughts), the {Scoring module} (verifies and scores the LLM thoughts), and the {Controller} (coordinates the entire reasoning process, and decides on how to progress it). % The Controller contains two further important elements: the Graph of Operations (GoO) and the Graph Reasoning State (GRS). GoO is a static structure that specifies the \emph{graph decomposition of a given task}, i.e., it prescribes transformations to be applied to LLM thoughts, together with their order \& dependencies. % GRS is a dynamic structure that maintains the state of the ongoing LLM reasoning process (the history of its thoughts and their states). % \if 0 Note that -- while we borrow this structure from the ToT work -- we extend it in two ways. First, we accommodate the general graph-based reasoning. Second, we ensure straightforward extendibility with new prompting schemes and use cases.  i \subsection{Prompter} The {Prompter} prepares the prompts to be sent to the LLM. This module is responsible for the specifics of encoding the graph structure within the prompt. % The 
ameAS architecture enables the user to implement use case specific graph encodings by providing full access to the graph structure. % % \maciej{Can you outline/summarize the way we do it, with concrete examples of the encoding, notation, etc.?} % % For example, in order to refer to specific graph vertices, in most cases, we use a simple monotonically increasing numbering of thoughts. \subsection{Parser} The {Parser} extracts information from LLM thoughts. For each such thought, the Parser constructs the \emph{thought state}, which contains this extracted information. The thought state is then used to update the GRS accordingly. % \subsection{Scoring \& Validation} Here, we verify whether a given LLM thought satisfies potential correctness conditions, and then we assign it a score. Depending on how the score is derived, the module may consult the LLM. Moreover, depending on the use case, the score may also be assigned by a human. Finally, use cases such as sorting use simple local scoring functions. \subsection{Controller} The {Controller} implements a specific strategy for selecting thoughts from its GRS structure. It also selects what transformations should be applied to which thoughts, and then passes this information to the Prompter. It also decides whether the whole process should be finalized, or whether the next round of interaction with the LLM should be initiated. In our current design, this is dictated by the execution plan specified in the GoO. \subsection{GoO \& GRS} The user constructs a GoO instance, which prescribes the execution plan of thought operations. The GoO is a static structure that is constructed once, before the execution starts. Each operation object knows its predecessor and successor operations. % Then, during the execution, an instance of the GRS maintains the continually updated information about the LLM reasoning process. This includes which operation has been executed so far, the states of all the generated LLM thoughts, their validity and scores, and any other relevant information. The above elements offer extensible 	extbf{APIs}, enabling straightforward implementations of different prompting schemes. The APIs are outlines 	extcolor{green}{in the green part} of Figure~ef{fig:arch}, and detailed in the documentation. We also provide 	extbf{examples of prompts} used by these operations and a corresponding GRS in the 	extcolor{red}{red part} of Figure~ef{fig:arch}. \if 0 The operations available to the user when constructing GoO are 	exttt{Generate(t,k)} (generate k new thoughts based on a thought t), 	exttt{Improve(t)} (enhance a thought t), 	exttt{Aggregate(t1,...,tk)} (combine thoughts t1, ..., tk), 	exttt{Score(t)} (score a thought t), 	exttt{Validate(t)} (validate the correctness of a thought t), and 	exttt{KeepBest(N)} (preserve N best scoring thoughts so far; the user can also specify the scope of thoughts to select from). Moreover, each operation can be instructed to generate multiple thoughts for the same input, for the purpose of obtaining more samples. For example, this enables 	exttt{Aggregate} to generate multiple combination outcomes. Each such outcome is recorded within GRS and scored individually. % Finally, to facilitate experiments, the user can provide LLM's input parameters (model used, temperature, max tokens, api keys, organization, etc.) and the LLM cost characteristics (prompt token cost, response token cost). The latter automatizes cost estimation when running experiments within 
